## 8. KNOWN UNKNOWNS — things I could not prove

### 8.1 Container-to-container reachability was read, not tested

`callmon_app` is on `[callmon-net, traefik-public]`; `ghl_clone_api` is on
`[ghl-net, traefik-public]`; `traefik-public` is `external: true` in both stacks. So
`http://ghl_clone_api:8000` should resolve by container name over the shared network,
internally, with nothing new exposed publicly.

**I read that out of both `docker-compose.prod.yml` files. I did not confirm it with a request
from inside a running container** — the sandbox I built in has no docker access (`docker ps`
is denied). `GET /api/crm-link/health` returns `crm_reachable` and exists precisely to settle
this in one command (§7 step 6). It is the first thing to check after enabling.

The related fact — that `callmon_worker` is on `callmon-net` only and therefore *cannot*
reach the CRM — is read from the same file, and is why the report hops through the app (§5.2).
That one is load-bearing: if I have it wrong, the hop is merely unnecessary, not broken.

### 8.2 A duplicate CRM row is possible, and I cannot fully close it

`POST /api/events` always INSERTs. It has no idempotency key and no dedupe on `provider_ref`.
So if the delivery POST succeeds but the response never reaches us (a timeout at exactly the
wrong moment), the job retries and the CRM gets a second row for the same call.

**What I did:** gave the whole delivery ONE budget (`CRM_LINK_HTTP_BUDGET_SECONDS=15`) that
sits inside the 20s client timeout `handle_crm_report` uses, so a slow CRM produces a clean
"budget exhausted" refusal rather than a worker-side timeout after the CRM has acted. Commit
`0deee92`; that bug was real and I introduced it — the original per-request timeout of 15s
across up to five calls could reach 75s.

**What I could not do:** make it idempotent. That needs a change on the CRM side (a unique
constraint on `provider_ref`, or an upsert), which is not mine to make. Every event carries
`calls.id` in `provider_ref`, so a duplicate is at least *identifiable* and de-duplicable.

### 8.3 The frontend typecheck did not run

`frontend/node_modules` is absent in this environment, so `scripts/check.sh` skipped `tsc`
— in my run **and in the clean-`origin/main` baseline**. I changed no frontend file, so there
is nothing for it to catch, but I did not run it and will not claim I did. `npm ci` in
`frontend/` re-enables it.

### 8.4 `bulkvs_client.send_message` has never been run against the live API

Flagging this as asked. `backend/app/providers/bulkvs_client.py:229` records, in the function
I reuse, that its `/messageSend` request shape **has never been exercised against the live
BulkVS API**. It follows the published docs (`From` = the DID, `To` = an array, `Message` =
the body). **It is not proven.** I did not change it, I did not test it against BulkVS, and
this module's SMS path inherits that uncertainty whole. When 10DLC is approved, the first send
is a test to a number you control, not a rollout.

### 8.5 No call has been placed

Nothing here has been exercised against a real Asterisk, a real ARI WebSocket, a real BulkVS
trunk, a real softphone or the running CRM. The ring group is tested against a fake ARI that
models the constraints I know about — including the ones that caused the live 2026-08-05
bridge failure — but a fake is a model of what I understood, and the standing lesson in
`tests/test_dial_record_bridge.py` is that a fake more permissive than reality hides exactly
the bug you care about. The verification checklist in `DEFAULT_CALL_HANDLING_SPEC.md` is the
right shape for a real-call test of this too.

### 8.6 `ring.py` calls two private methods on `AsteriskAriClient`

`_await_first_answer`, `_await_bridge_end`, `_post_json` and `_delete`. Deliberate:
`_await_first_answer` in particular carries hard-won live-call fixes (StasisStart vs
`ChannelStateChange`→Up, caller-hangup-while-ringing, per-leg `ChannelDestroyed` pruning), and
forking it would freeze that knowledge at today's date and silently miss the next fix. The
cost is that renaming or changing those methods breaks this module — and `test_crm_hybrid_ring`
is what would tell you, because its fake implements them by name.

### 8.7 Things I did not build, because you scoped them out

Inbound SMS from the CRM's side, contact sync, opportunity creation, the `Opportunity`
`custom_fields.owen_call_id` write itself (OWEN *sends* `calls.id`; storing it as
`owen_call_id` is the CRM's half), and the AI agent (§7.2). Phase 1 is voice.

