## 9. BUGS I FOUND IN MY OWN CODE WHILE TESTING IT

Listed separately because they are the most useful part of a report like this.

1. **`93c7c99` — the allowlist split on whitespace.** `+1 561-555-0111` — the most ordinary way
   a person writes a phone number — became the two useless keys `"1"` and `"5615550111"`. The
   destination you meant to allow would have been silently absent and a call you expected to go
   out would not have. That is the exact failure the guard exists to prevent, arriving through
   the guard itself. Commas, semicolons and newlines separate now; a space is part of a number.

2. **`0deee92` — one delivery could exceed the worker's HTTP timeout.** Up to five calls out at
   15s each against a 20s client timeout. See §8.2.

3. **`34f5be1` — the unwired agent seam would have reported every agent call as `failed`.**
   `handler` sets `outcome="agent"`, `crm_call_status` had no entry for it, and the
   unknown-outcome default is `"failed"`. The day somebody wires the agent, the CRM's call
   report would have said the phone system was broken. Two lines to name it now.

4. **`887623b` — the foreign key would have made a `DELETE FROM numbers` newly fail.** With
   the default `NO ACTION`, a row in my table would block a delete on an existing table.
   `ON DELETE CASCADE` fixes it. Measured both ways rather than reasoned about (§4.3).

And one that is **NOT mine, and which I did not touch** — flagging it because it is a live
landmine in the startup path: `alembic revision --autogenerate` on this repo proposes three
`SET NOT NULL` ALTERs and two `DROP INDEX`es against `billing_adjustments`, `billing_rates`
and `call_charges`. Since migrations run automatically at app startup, accepting that output
would ship them into the boot path, where a `SET NOT NULL` that hits an existing NULL fails
and the app does not come back up. Full detail and my reasoning for refusing it: **§4.4**.

